|  needleDBD -- ONE place for every number in this case.                      |
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// HOW THIS FILE WORKS, and its one sharp edge.
//
// Every dictionary in the case reaches this file with
//     #include "../configuration/config"
// and refers to a value as $name. That is a CASE CONVENTION, not an OpenFOAM
// feature: a value defined here that no dictionary references DOES NOTHING, and
// nothing warns you. Four of the beds in this repository carry such variables --
// `appliedVoltage` among them, which is exactly the knob you would reach for to
// change the voltage.
//
// So the rule for this file is: if it is here, something reads it. To check:
//     for k in $(grep -oE '^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*' configuration/config | sort -u); do
//         grep -rqF "\$$k" system/ constant/ etc/ 0.orig/ Allrun-serial \
//             || echo "DANGLING: $k"
//     done


// ===========================================================================
//  APPLIED VOLTAGE -- NOT HERE
// ===========================================================================
//
// The drive lives on the PATCH it drives, in etc/changeDictionary.<gasRegion>:
//
//     active_electrode
//     {
//         type            uniformFixedValue;
//         uniformValue    table ((0 0) (100e-9 8e3));
//         value           uniform 0;
//     }
//
// There is no case variable for it, deliberately, for two reasons.
//
// IT IS A PER-PATCH PROPERTY. It applies only to electrodes that are NOT
// grounded -- `ground` has no waveform -- so a global variable would be a
// global name for a quantity that is local, the same mistake as a single
// `seeCoefficient` for surfaces made of different materials.
//
// AND THE CONTEXT ALREADY NAMES IT. `uniformValue` is OpenFOAM's own key on
// `uniformFixedValue`, and the patch name says which electrode. A variable
// would add a name where the surroundings already supply one -- and the name it
// used to have, `voltageRamp`, was actively misleading: it named ONE
// implementation of the intent, while the value is any Function1 and is a
// `sine`, `square`, `csv` or `constant` more often than a ramp.
//
// The Function1 menu and worked examples (AC, damped AC, pulses, a measured
// waveform from CSV) are in docs/reference/changeDictionary.md.



// ===========================================================================
//  GAS STATE -- stated once, here
// ===========================================================================

// 1 bar, not 1 atm. The two differ by 1.3% in density, which shifts E/N and
// therefore every rate coefficient; small, but not the kind of difference to
// leave unexplained.
//
// The gas NUMBER DENSITY is not stated anywhere: plasmaSpecies closes it from
// this pressure and this temperature by the ideal gas law, and pushes it out as
// the one owner. A second copy of the density is how E/N came to be evaluated
// at 1 atm in a case that was at 1 bar.
gasPressure                         1e5;      // [Pa]
gasTemperature                      300;      // [K]

// PRESSURE IS STATED ONCE, HERE, IN PASCAL.
//
// It used to be written three times in two files in two units:
// `backgroundGas/pressure` [Pa], `backgroundGas/energy/pressure` [Pa] and
// `boltzmann/pressureAtm` [atm]. All three are now one: the nested duplicate is
// gone and the sweep density is DERIVED, because a three-body process breaks
// E/N similarity so there is exactly ONE correct density for the sweep -- the
// one the case runs at. `pressureAtm` is rejected with the derived value shown.
//
// Anything PRINTED shows Pa first with the conversions after, e.g.
//     100000 Pa (0.986923 atm, 750.062 Torr, 1 bar)


// ===========================================================================
//  GEOMETRY AND MATERIALS
// ===========================================================================

// PERMITTIVITY IS NOT HERE, deliberately.
//
// Each region's epsilonR lives in its own constant/<region>/electricalProperties,
// which is the OpenFOAM convention (the same place and shape chtMultiRegionFoam
// keeps constant/<region>/thermophysicalProperties) and the whole point of the
// region/material layout: adding a region means adding a DIRECTORY, and nothing
// global is edited.
//
// Declaring it here as well would put one value in two homes -- exactly the
// duplication that was removed when constant/electromagneticsProperties was
// deleted. tools/plasmaSetupRegions.sh writes those files with literal values
// for the same reason: a generated template that depends on a config variable
// existing is fragile.
//
//     constant/gas/electricalProperties          epsilonR 1.0;
//     constant/dielectric/electricalProperties   epsilonR 3.0;   <- set this

// REGION COUPLING -- NOT HERE either.
//
// It is a PER-INTERFACE property: `useImplicit` sits on each
// `coupledElectricPotential` patch, so a three-region stack could legitimately
// couple one interface monolithically and another segregated. It lives in
// etc/changeDictionary.<region>, on the interface patch:
//
//     gas_to_dielectric
//     {
//         type            coupledElectricPotential;
//         useImplicit     true;     // MONOLITHIC: one matrix for both regions
//         surfCharge      surfCharge;
//         surfChargeNbr   none;
//     }
//
// WHY THE KEY IS STILL CALLED `useImplicit`, having been renamed here. It is
// OPENFOAM'S OWN name for the concept: `fvPatchField` carries a `useImplicit_`
// member and a `useImplicit()` accessor, and -- decisively --
// `fvPatchField::write` EMITS `useImplicit true` whenever the flag is set. So
// accepting a better name would mean reading one spelling and writing another:
// you would type `monolithicCoupling` and find `useImplicit` in
// 0/gas/ePotential afterwards. Two spellings for one setting is the exact
// defect removed from poissonSolver/PoissonScheme and
// limitVoltageRiseRate/maxVoltageRiseRate.
//
// And on a PATCH the name is precise, which it was not as a global case
// variable: it says whether this coupling contributes to the MATRIX or is
// lagged as a source. What was wrong was one global `useImplicit` in a solver
// where the chemistry source, the wall flux and the Poisson scheme each have
// their own implicit/explicit choice.
//
// See docs/reference/changeDictionary.md for the measured cost of the lag.


// surfChargeDict is GONE. The field name was never a choice -- the solver
// registers it as the literal "surfCharge" -- so the variable had one valid
// value and every other value silently meant "no surface charge", the BC doing
// `if (surfChargeName_ != "none")` and quietly finding nothing.
//
// The real decision is WHICH SIDE OWNS IT, and that is the pairing across the
// two etc/changeDictionary files, where both halves are visible together:
//     .gas         surfCharge surfCharge;  surfChargeNbr none;
//     .dielectric  surfCharge none;        surfChargeNbr surfCharge;
// Exactly one side owns it or it is double-counted.

// SEE IS NOT HERE. It is a per-SURFACE property, so it lives in the boundary
// conditions: `defaultSEEC` on the species patch fields (n_e and nEps_e) in
// etc/changeDictionary.<region>, one value per emitting patch.
//
// A single case variable would force every emitting surface to share a value,
// which is wrong for this geometry: a metal needle and a dielectric barrier are
// different materials with different gamma. And dielectrics emit too -- SEE is
// not an electrode-only phenomenon.
//
// The solver defaults it to 0.001 (contaminated oxide / barrier), so a patch
// that does not state one is not silently given a clean-metal figure.

// THE INITIAL CONDITION, not a numerical guard.
//
// This case has NO SEEDED BLOB. The discharge initiates from this background
// density in the geometrically enhanced field at the needle tip, so the value
// is physics.
//
// IMPLICATION: a clamp is a source. Electrons are injected wherever attachment
// would have removed them, so this only conserves charge because the positive
// ions are clamped to the same total, split by mole fraction. Raise one and you
// must raise the others.


// ===========================================================================
//  DISCRETISATION SCHEMES
// ===========================================================================

driftDiffusionFluxScheme            standard;

electronDriftDivScheme                  Gauss ROUNDF;
electronDiffusionLaplacianScheme        Gauss harmonic corrected 0.33;
electronMobilityInterpolationScheme     harmonic;
electronDiffusivityInterpolationScheme  harmonic;
ePotentialLaplacianScheme               Gauss linear corrected 0.33;


// ===========================================================================
//  LINEAR SOLVERS
// ===========================================================================

// THE LINEAR-SOLVER BACKEND, IN ONE PLACE.
//
// Other beds in this repository ship TWO whole dictionaries --
// `system/fvSolution-foam` and `system/fvSolution-petsc` -- and their Allrun
// copies one over `system/fvSolution` according to a `matrixSolver` variable.
// That makes `system/fvSolution` a build artefact whose edits vanish silently,
// and it duplicates every unrelated setting (species tolerances, PIMPLE
// controls) across two files that then drift.
//
// It is unnecessary. MEASURED 2026-09-02: an OpenFOAM solver dictionary
// SILENTLY IGNORES entries it does not use -- a `PCG` block carrying
// `agglomerator`, `nCellsInCoarsestLevel` and a `petsc { options { ... } }`
// sub-dictionary runs clean. So one `ePotential` block can carry BOTH
// backends' settings, and switching backend is one word here.
//
//   GAMG        native, needs `agglomerator assembledFaceAreaPair` under
//               monolithic coupling (see system/fvSolution)
//   PBiCGStab   native, for a non-symmetric operator
//   petsc       PETSc, configured by the `petsc { options { ... } }` block
//
ePotentialSolver                    GAMG;

// The libraries controlDict loads, as ONE WHOLE VALUE so the list can be
// swapped from here. ROUNDSchemes supplies the `Gauss ROUNDF` divergence
// scheme the electron equation uses.
//
// TO USE PETSc: set ePotentialSolver above to `petsc` and add
// "libpetscFoam.so" to this list. Both are in this file, so no dictionary
// moves and nothing is copied.
//
// NOT LOADED BY DEFAULT, and on this machine that is not merely caution:
// libpetscFoam.so is installed but cannot load --
//     Could not load "libpetscFoam.so"
//     libpetsc.so.3.24: cannot open shared object file
// The wrapper is present, PETSc itself is not. It is a WARNING rather than a
// fatal error, so an unnecessary entry costs a confusing line at start-up on
// every run.

// A SCHEME, not a linear solver -- hence the name. `explicit` treats the space
// charge as a pure source; `semiImplicit` also brings the conductivity in
// implicitly, which stabilises the space-charge / Poisson coupling. The LINEAR
// solver for ePotential is the `ePotential` block in fvSolution, below.
//
// This key used to be called `poissonSolver` while the dictionary entry it fed
// was called `PoissonScheme`: two names for one setting, each suggesting the
// other's meaning.

// FOUR VALUES, NOT TEN NAMES.
//
// This was ePotential/ne/npIon x Tolerance/RelTolerance plus two PIMPLE
// variants: ten names carrying four distinct numbers, so ten places to edit and
// nine ways for them to drift apart. `relTol 0` in particular is not a tuning
// choice -- it means "converge on the absolute tolerance" -- and it is the same
// decision for every field here.
//
// REVERSIBLE. fvSolution's named entry beats a catch-all, so a field that later
// needs its own tolerance gets a variable back at that point rather than one
// being carried speculatively.
linearTol                           1e-10;    // ePotential, n_e, all species
linearRelTol                        0.0;      // absolute convergence, all fields

// The two genuine outliers, kept separate because their values really differ.
                                              // tolerance, and MUST stay >= 10x
                                              // the linear tolerance or the
                                              // outer loop can never meet it
outerTol                            1e-10;    // n_e and species, outer loop


// ===========================================================================
//  TIME CONTROL
// ===========================================================================


// 3 ns, not 20. MEASURED 2026-09-02: this case runs at ~2.8 s/step, so the
// 2e-08 it used to ask for is about a THREE-HOUR run -- not a walkthrough.
//
// 3 ns is sized to the observable rather than to a round number. With ~15x tip
// enhancement E/N reaches the ionisation range at roughly V ~ 167 V, i.e.
// t ~ 2.1 ns on an 8 kV / 100 ns ramp, so 3 ns crosses initiation with margin
// and finishes in well under an hour.
//
// RAISE IT to 2e-08 for the full physics: the barrier charging that makes a DBD
// self-limiting happens over tens of ns, and 3 ns shows initiation only.
//
// The interesting window opens EARLY. With ~15x tip enhancement, E/N reaches
// the ionisation range at roughly V ~ 167 V, i.e. t ~ 2.1 ns on an 8 kV /
// 100 ns ramp -- long before the ramp completes. Write often enough to see it.

// Timestep limiters. maxSpeciesCo is a RAIL rather than the control: on this
// bed the binding limiter is the ENERGY convective Courant, which shares this
// cap.
maxSpeciesCo                        100;


// limitDielectricRelaxationRatio is NOT declared: false is already the default.
// The ratio is still REPORTED (printDielectricRelaxationRatio below), compared
// against maxDielectricRelaxationRatio -- which is the right way round, since a
// limiter should be measured before it is switched on.

// ONE key for the voltage rise: volts per step, 0 disables, always active when
// a `voltagePatchName` is named. 100 V/step over an 8 kV ramp is ~80 points
// across the rise.
//
// There is no separate on/off switch and no separate print switch -- reporting
// follows the limiter. `limitVoltageRiseRate` and `maxVoltageRiseRate` are
// REJECTED with an error carrying the exact translation for your dictionary,
// and `printVoltageRiseRate` prints a notice saying it is no longer read. Four
// keys once described this one quantity, which is how a case sets one and is
// surprised by another.
// maxVoltageRisePerStep is NOT declared: 100 V/step is already the code default
// (plasmaTimeControl.C), and restating a default makes it look like the knob to
// tune while the reasoning for the value lives at the reader. It limits deltaT
// so the applied voltage changes by at most that much per step; it is active
// once `voltagePatchName` names the driven electrode, inert at constant voltage
// where dV/step ~ 0, and 0 disables it. The run reports when it binds.

// ************************************************************************* //

// TRANSIENT or PSEUDO-STEADY. `transient` is real time-marching -- what every
// shipped tutorial does. `pseudoSteady` is PSEUDO-TRANSIENT CONTINUATION for
// reaching a stationary state: `deltaT` becomes a RELAXATION PARAMETER, the
// time axis stops being physical, and adjustTimeStep is forced off so the step
// cannot collapse. It also interlocks several settings across three files, so
// it is set HERE and enforced in the solver -- see doc/steady-mode-spec.md.
//
// `steadyState` is deliberately NOT offered: dropping ddt leaves a segregated
// species equation with no matrix diagonal, and the linear solver divides by
// zero on its first sweep.
simulationType                      transient;

